OPEN DEFECT: chroma inter prediction when the LUMA mv is integer-pel but the
CHROMA mv is half-pel — 2026-09-10

SIGNATURE, and it is exact. At 4:2:0 a motion vector is eighth-pel for luma and
effectively sixteenth-pel for chroma:
    luma   phase = (mv.col * 2) & 15
    chroma phase =  mv.col      & 15
So an mv.col that is an ODD multiple of 8 is INTEGER in luma and HALF-PEL in
chroma. The port gets luma right and chroma wrong in exactly that case.

MEASURED, vidyo3 256x256 p6 qp34 frames=2, frame 1, every inter block in the
frame classified:

  luma_integer  chroma_subpel   blocks   containing a differing pixel
  ------------------------------------------------------------------
  false         true              107     0
  true          false              16     0
  true          true                3     2

107 ordinary sub-pel blocks are perfect and 16 fully-integer blocks are perfect;
only the 3-block class fails. The two failing blocks are 16x16 with
mv=(0,-8): luma phase (-16 & 15) = 0, chroma phase (-8 & 15) = 8.

WHY THIS IS A CLEAN REPRODUCER
- Frame 1's reference is the KEY frame, which is byte-identical on both sides.
- At qp34 this cell signals loop_filter_level[0] = 0. Both libaom
  (check_planes_to_loop_filter BREAKS when both luma levels are zero) and the
  port skip ALL planes, so no filter stage runs on either side.
- LUMA is byte-identical; only U and V differ, by +-1..2.
So: same reference, same MV, no filters, luma right, chroma wrong.

RULED OUT by reading source, each one a plausible candidate first:
- The sub-pel derivation. port_subpel_params::clamp_mv_to_umv_border_sb does
  `src_mv.x * (1 << (1 - ss_x))`, so ss_x=1 yields phase 8. Correct.
- The chroma call site. port_pd_pred.rs's CHROMA_MASK branch passes ss_y=1,
  ss_x=1 to compute_subpel_params, with the LUMA bw/bh for the clamp (which is
  what C does) and the halved origin. Correct.
- The convolve dispatch. dispatch_convolve_8 keys on (subpel_x != 0,
  subpel_y != 0) per plane, so a chroma phase of 8 selects convolve_x, not the
  copy path. Correct.
- The loop filter, per the qp34 evidence above.
- skip_inter: forcing it false changes the chroma result NOT AT ALL.
- CDEF: refuted outright — vidyo3 drifts with CDEF 0 on every frame, vidyo1
  drifts BEFORE CDEF turns on, and vidyo4 is clean WITH a CDEF transition.
- MFMV as the sole cause: disabling it only in the header desynchronises from
  frame 0 (the port builds the field unconditionally), so that experiment was
  invalid rather than confirming.

WHERE TO LOOK NEXT. The MD path (inter_md_arm -> predict_inter_yuv ->
av1_inter_prediction_light_pd1) is correct at every layer checked above, so the
final RECON chroma is likely produced by a different route than the one audited.
Find what writes the reconstructed chroma for an inter leaf and check whether it
recomputes the phase from the luma MV instead of reusing the chroma SubpelParams.

The third block of the class does not show a differing pixel; a half-pel filter
over a locally flat chroma region can equal the copy, so that is expected and
not evidence against the signature.

================================================================
ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND FIXED, same day
================================================================

The interpolation-filter search picks a filter pair, assigns it to the
candidate (so it is SIGNALLED), and then rebuilds the prediction only when
`res.invalidates_luma_pred` is true. But THE SEARCH PREDICTS LUMA ONLY, so the
chroma buffers still hold the prediction made with the filters the injector set
-- packed 0, EIGHTTAP_REGULAR both ways -- no matter which pair wins.
`invalidates_luma_pred` answers a different question: whether the LUMA buffer
happens to already hold the winning pair's prediction because it was tried last.
Gating the chroma rebuild on it leaves chroma predicted with a filter the
bitstream does not name.

INSTRUMENTED PROOF, block (16,160) 16x16 of vidyo3 256x256 p6 qp34 frame 1:
    ZZIFS best_filters=0x10001 was=0x0 invalidates_luma=false has_uv=true
0x10001 is SMOOTH/SMOOTH; 0x0 is REGULAR/REGULAR. Nothing was rebuilt, so the
recon kept REGULAR chroma while the header said SMOOTH. A probe of the actual
predicted row confirmed it:
    port pred row0 = [126,126,126,127,123,119,119,120]  (filters=0x0)
    decoder row0   = [126,126,126,125,123,120,119,119]

WHY IT HID FOR SO LONG. It needs the LUMA phase to be zero -- an integer luma MV
applies no filter at all, so luma is right whichever pair is used -- while the
CHROMA phase is non-zero. At 4:2:0 that is exactly an mv component that is an
odd multiple of 8. Every ordinary sub-pel block rebuilds or is unaffected, which
is why 107 of them were perfect and only the 3-block class failed.

FIX: rebuild when `invalidates_luma_pred || (has_uv && best_filters != org)`.
Safe for luma: with `invalidates_luma_pred` false the luma buffer already holds
this pair's prediction, so re-running it is idempotent.

RESULT (8 frames, encoder recon vs aomdec, all six clips):
                    qp40 before          qp40 after
  johnny            drift f2 (9 px)      ALL 8 CLEAN
  fourpeople        ALL 8 CLEAN          ALL 8 CLEAN
  kristenandsara    ALL 8 CLEAN          ALL 8 CLEAN
  vidyo1            drift f1             drift f4   (3 frames further)
  vidyo3            drift f3             drift f3
  vidyo4            4/8 decoded          4/8 decoded

Three of six clips now reconstruct byte-identically to the decoder for all eight
frames. NO REGRESSION: inter_byte_gate 108/108, ifs_join_gate 96/96 (the port's
MDS3 filter search against C's, the gate that covers this exact code),
real_video_inter_gate 24/24.

STILL OPEN: vidyo3 f3, vidyo1 f4, vidyo4 f4, and at qp20 several clips drift at
f2. Those are separate defects -- this one is closed.
